{"entity": "Metformin", "type": "artifact", "description": "Metformin is an oral antidiabetic medication belonging to the biguanide class that serves as the primary, first-line treatment for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, recommended prior to initiating therapies such as SGLT2 inhibitors. It is also utilized as a first-line therapeutic agent in the management of insulin resistance. In addition to being administered as a standalone therapy, Metformin is an active component of the combination drug Zituvimet.\n\nThe primary therapeutic function of Metformin is to effectively lower blood glucose levels. It achieves this through a dual mechanism of action that involves improving peripheral insulin sensitivity and reducing hepatic glucose output. In terms of pharmacokinetics, the clearance of Metformin from the body can be significantly influenced by specific drug interactions, requiring careful management when co-administered with certain medications.\n\nPatient renal function plays a critical role in the safe administration of Metformin. Dosage adjustments are required based on a patient's estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR), and the drug is strictly contraindicated in patients with chronic kidney disease who have an eGFR of less than 30 mL/min/1.73 m². Additionally, Metformin carries a risk of lactic acidosis; to minimize this hazard, the medication must be temporarily withheld prior to medical procedures involving the administration of iodinated contrast agents.<SEP>A pharmaceutical drug and first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes that exhibits multiple health benefits, including promoting weight loss and mitigating metabolic disorders.<SEP>A medication used to treat insulin resistance, compared against dietary interventions regarding weight loss and insulin regulation in PCOS.<SEP>A pharmacological agent associated with the prevention and management of type 2 diabetes mellitus.<SEP>A biguanide oral hypoglycaemic tablet used as a first-line treatment for type 2 diabetes."}
{"entity": "Aging", "type": "concept", "description": "A biological process whose associated consequences and metabolic diseases can be reversed or managed by metformin."}
{"entity": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus", "type": "concept", "description": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels and inadequate blood sugar control. Insulin resistance plays a primary and determining pathophysiological role in the onset and progression of the disease. \n\nManagement of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus relies on a combination of lifestyle interventions, including diet and exercise, alongside tailored pharmacological therapies to improve glycemic control. Metformin is established as the first-line treatment for the condition. Should further intervention be required, Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors are recommended as a second-line treatment following metformin. Additionally, agents such as sitagliptin and Zituvimet—which is indicated specifically for adult patients—are prescribed to optimize blood glucose management in conjunction with diet, exercise, and metformin regimens.\n\nTherapeutic approaches for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus also target associated cardiovascular and renal comorbidities to minimize complications. Patients with coronary heart disease (CHD) risk factors benefit from treatment with Atorvastatin Calcium, which effectively reduces the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke. Furthermore, in patients who present with renal complications such as albuminuria, the administration of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors (ACEIs) or Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs) results in a significant decline in albuminuria levels.<SEP>A chronic metabolic condition characterized by elevated blood sugar levels, which can lead to life-threatening complications, reduced quality of life, and higher healthcare costs."}
{"entity": "Metabolic Diseases", "type": "concept", "description": "A group of health conditions affecting normal metabolism, often associated with aging and targetable by metformin pathways."}
