{"entity": "Type 2 Diabetes", "type": "concept", "description": "Type 2 Diabetes is a metabolic disorder characterized by high blood sugar and insulin resistance, evaluated in clinical trials for ADMELOG.<SEP>A metabolic disorder serving as an inclusion criterion for patients in the VA NEPHRON trial.<SEP>A metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels, for which insulin resistance is a key feature.<SEP>A metabolic condition characterized by insulin resistance combined with progressive beta-cell dysfunction, treated through a stepwise approach.<SEP>A metabolic disorder for which metformin serves as a primary first-line treatment."}
{"entity": "Hepatic Steatosis", "type": "concept", "description": "A liver disease characterized by excessive fat accumulation, identified as a sequela of obesity explored for management by metformin."}
{"entity": "Amlodipine", "type": "artifact", "description": "A medication whose systemic exposure increases when co-administered with CYP3A inhibitors, and which increases the systemic exposure of simvastatin, cyclosporine, and tacrolimus.<SEP>A medication used to treat cardiovascular conditions that can cause adverse reactions such as edema, hypotension, worsening angina, and acute myocardial infarction.<SEP>A long-acting dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker used primarily for treating hypertension and angina.<SEP>A calcium channel blocker tablet used as an antihypertensive medicine."}
{"entity": "Gut Microbiome", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "The ecological community of microorganisms in the digestive tract, which is altered by metformin therapy."}
{"entity": "Hypertension", "type": "concept", "description": "Hypertension is a chronic medical condition characterized by persistently elevated or high blood pressure. If left unmanaged, the condition increases the overall risk of severe cardiovascular events. Additionally, in individuals diagnosed with non-valvular atrial fibrillation, hypertension significantly heightens the risk of stroke. It also plays a major role in complicating other health conditions, including chronic kidney disease and heart failure.\n\nThe management of hypertension relies on a combination of lifestyle interventions and targeted antihypertensive drug therapies designed to effectively lower blood pressure. Commonly prescribed therapeutic agents include amlodipine (and its formulation amlodipine besylate) as well as metoprolol tartrate. Treatment strategies are often tailored based on underlying comorbidities; for example, ACE inhibitors serve as first-line therapeutic agents when managing hypertension in patients with chronic kidney disease. Similarly, for patients with hypertension who also suffer from heart failure, carvedilol is slightly better suited as a treatment option compared to metoprolol."}
