{"entity": "Heart Failure", "type": "concept", "description": "Heart Failure is a cardiac and cardiovascular condition that significantly influences clinical decision-making regarding pharmacotherapy, particularly in the choice and management of specific antihypertensive medications. Certain antihypertensive drugs carry additional approved indications and deliver direct therapeutic benefits for patients with heart failure.\n\nThe condition is sensitive to various drug therapies and interactions that can increase its incidence or exacerbate symptoms. The risk of heart failure increases with the use of non-aspirin nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), such as ibuprofen. Furthermore, heart failure can be induced or worsened by fluid retention when thiazolidinediones are prescribed concomitantly with insulin. It has also been observed in connection with the DPP-4 inhibitor class of medications, requiring healthcare providers to conduct thorough risk and benefit evaluations prior to initiating and throughout treatment with drugs such as Zituvimet.\n\nIn the context of beta-blocker therapy, heart failure was evaluated in myocardial infarction (MI) trials, and its exacerbation is recognized as a reported adverse reaction to metoprolol tartrate. The condition may particularly worsen during the up-titration phase of metoprolol tartrate, necessitating close clinical monitoring and potential dose adjustments to ensure patient safety.<SEP>A cardiac condition characterized by impaired ventricular function, acting as a stroke risk factor in atrial fibrillation patients.<SEP>A clinical condition characterized by impaired cardiac function in which beta-blockers serve as a cornerstone treatment.<SEP>A cardiovascular condition in which mortality can be reduced by treatment with metoprolol succinate.<SEP>A cardiovascular condition in which the therapeutic combination of an ACE inhibitor and spironolactone provides a proven mortality benefit.<SEP>A cardiac condition for which SGLT2 inhibitors offer therapeutic benefit by reducing worsening events or cardiovascular death.<SEP>A health condition (HF) for which beta-blockers are now a recommended and preferred therapeutic approach, particularly in patients with reduced ejection fraction."}
