{"entity": "Metformin", "type": "artifact", "description": "Metformin is an oral antidiabetic medication belonging to the biguanide class that serves as the primary, first-line treatment for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, recommended prior to initiating therapies such as SGLT2 inhibitors. It is also utilized as a first-line therapeutic agent in the management of insulin resistance. In addition to being administered as a standalone therapy, Metformin is an active component of the combination drug Zituvimet.\n\nThe primary therapeutic function of Metformin is to effectively lower blood glucose levels. It achieves this through a dual mechanism of action that involves improving peripheral insulin sensitivity and reducing hepatic glucose output. In terms of pharmacokinetics, the clearance of Metformin from the body can be significantly influenced by specific drug interactions, requiring careful management when co-administered with certain medications.\n\nPatient renal function plays a critical role in the safe administration of Metformin. Dosage adjustments are required based on a patient's estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR), and the drug is strictly contraindicated in patients with chronic kidney disease who have an eGFR of less than 30 mL/min/1.73 m². Additionally, Metformin carries a risk of lactic acidosis; to minimize this hazard, the medication must be temporarily withheld prior to medical procedures involving the administration of iodinated contrast agents.<SEP>A pharmaceutical drug and first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes that exhibits multiple health benefits, including promoting weight loss and mitigating metabolic disorders.<SEP>A medication used to treat insulin resistance, compared against dietary interventions regarding weight loss and insulin regulation in PCOS.<SEP>A pharmacological agent associated with the prevention and management of type 2 diabetes mellitus.<SEP>A biguanide oral hypoglycaemic tablet used as a first-line treatment for type 2 diabetes."}
