{"entity": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus", "type": "concept", "description": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels and inadequate blood sugar control. Insulin resistance plays a primary and determining pathophysiological role in the onset and progression of the disease. \n\nManagement of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus relies on a combination of lifestyle interventions, including diet and exercise, alongside tailored pharmacological therapies to improve glycemic control. Metformin is established as the first-line treatment for the condition. Should further intervention be required, Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors are recommended as a second-line treatment following metformin. Additionally, agents such as sitagliptin and Zituvimet—which is indicated specifically for adult patients—are prescribed to optimize blood glucose management in conjunction with diet, exercise, and metformin regimens.\n\nTherapeutic approaches for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus also target associated cardiovascular and renal comorbidities to minimize complications. Patients with coronary heart disease (CHD) risk factors benefit from treatment with Atorvastatin Calcium, which effectively reduces the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke. Furthermore, in patients who present with renal complications such as albuminuria, the administration of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors (ACEIs) or Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs) results in a significant decline in albuminuria levels.<SEP>A chronic metabolic condition characterized by elevated blood sugar levels, which can lead to life-threatening complications, reduced quality of life, and higher healthcare costs."}
