{"entity": "Diarrhea", "type": "concept", "description": "Diarrhea is a widely recognized gastrointestinal adverse reaction, side effect, and symptom associated with various pharmacological treatments and medication regimens evaluated across clinical trials and general medical practice.\n\nIt is frequently documented in patients undergoing cardiovascular and lipid-lowering therapies. In clinical trials for Atorvastatin Calcium, diarrhea was observed as a common gastrointestinal adverse reaction. It also presents as a gastrointestinal side effect in cardiovascular management, occurring in approximately 5% of patients taking metoprolol tartrate, as well as in 2.5% of patients treated with lisinopril and hydrochlorothiazide tablets in clinical trials.\n\nIn the management of metabolic conditions, diarrhea is a well-established side effect associated with the use of metformin, both as a standalone therapy and when coadministered in combination with sitagliptin. Clinical trials and patient reports for sitagliptin and metformin combination therapy consistently highlight diarrhea as a common adverse reaction. Furthermore, in the context of gastrointestinal therapies, diarrhea serves as a notable symptom or adverse effect that explicitly requires patients to stop using Omeprazole and consult a healthcare professional."}
