{"entity": "ACE Inhibitors", "type": "concept", "description": "ACE Inhibitors are a class of cardiovascular medications that inhibit the angiotensin-converting enzyme, thereby altering the metabolism of bradykinin and eicosanoids while reducing aldosterone secretion. Common medications belonging to this class include lisinopril, enalapril, and ramipril. ACE Inhibitors are primarily utilized to lower blood pressure and slow the progression of renal disease.\n\nWhile effective, ACE Inhibitors carry several risks and drug interactions. By increasing serum potassium levels, they can trigger hyperkalemia or cause acute kidney injury, particularly when combined with other therapeutic agents. They are also associated with the risk of developing angioedema. Furthermore, when concomitantly administered with diabetes treatments such as Admelog (ADMELOG), ACE Inhibitors may increase the risk of hypoglycemia. Consequently, treatment guidelines often recommend initiating therapy at low initial doses and closely monitoring serum creatinine and potassium levels, particularly in patients suffering from chronic kidney disease.<SEP>Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, a therapeutic intervention used to treat patients with chronic kidney disease.<SEP>A class of medications, also known as angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, used to treat cardiovascular and renal conditions by reducing albuminuria progression."}
