{"entity": "Aspirin", "type": "artifact", "description": "Aspirin, also known as acetylsalicylic acid, is a widely used nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and antiplatelet medication. It functions by irreversibly inhibiting cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) in platelets, thereby reducing blood clotting. As a general therapeutic drug, Aspirin is commonly utilized as a pain reliever and fever reducer to provide temporary relief from minor aches and pains.\n\nIn cardiovascular and neurological care, Aspirin plays a central role as an antiplatelet agent and is frequently incorporated into combination therapies. It serves as a monotherapy baseline when evaluating treatment strategies against dual antiplatelet therapy for acute ischemic stroke and transient ischemic attack. Additionally, Aspirin has been evaluated as a standalone single antiplatelet strategy following procedures such as transcatheter aortic valve implantation.\n\nDespite its clinical benefits, Aspirin carries notable risks and drug interactions. Concomitant administration with other NSAIDs, direct oral anticoagulants, or traditional anticoagulant therapy such as warfarin additively increases bleeding risks, specifically amplifying the likelihood of gastrointestinal bleeding. Furthermore, taking Aspirin alongside ibuprofen can heighten the risk of allergic reactions and potentially reduce the cardiovascular benefits offered by Aspirin.<SEP>A medicine used as an antiplatelet antithrombotic agent at low doses and as an NSAID non-opioid analgesic."}
