{"entity": "Ibuprofen", "type": "artifact", "description": "Ibuprofen is a non-selective nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and non-opioid analgesic commonly administered in tablet form. It is primarily indicated for the temporary relief of minor aches and pains, serving as an analgesic, and reducing fever.\n\nAlthough Ibuprofen carries notable gastrointestinal risks, such as damaging the gastrointestinal mucosa and increasing the potential for gastrointestinal bleeding, it is associated with the lowest significant risk of gastrointestinal bleeding among the non-selective NSAIDs evaluated. Beyond its analgesic and antipyretic functions, Ibuprofen possesses antiplatelet effects that impair normal platelet function.\n\nBecause of these pharmacological properties, Ibuprofen carries specific product warnings concerning concurrent medication use. When taken alongside warfarin, Ibuprofen increases the overall risk of bleeding through a combination of gastrointestinal mucosal damage and platelet impairment. Furthermore, if taken prior to aspirin, Ibuprofen can interfere with aspirin's antiplatelet effect while also heightening the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding."}
