{"entity": "Hypoglycemia", "type": "concept", "description": "Hypoglycemia is a potentially life-threatening medical condition characterized by abnormally low levels of blood glucose, or blood sugar. It frequently occurs as a result of insulin administration during diabetes management. While receiving metformin alone as a monotherapy does not cause hypoglycemia, the condition can occur when medications such as Zituvimet are coadministered or used in combination with insulin or insulin secretagogues.\n\nIn specific clinical contexts, hypoglycemia represents the most common adverse reaction associated with ADMELOG (Admelog), and active hypoglycemic episodes serve as a contraindication for Admelog administration. Additionally, the clinical warning signs and symptoms of hypoglycemia in diabetic patients can be masked by treatment with metoprolol tartrate. However, metoprolol masks these low blood sugar symptoms to a lesser extent than non-selective beta-blockers do.<SEP>A low blood sugar condition for which SGLT2 inhibitor therapy carries a low risk.<SEP>A condition of abnormally low blood sugar in diabetic patients, whose sympathetic symptoms like tachycardia can be hidden by beta-blockers."}
