{"entity": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus", "type": "concept", "description": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus is a chronic metabolic disorder characterized by high blood glucose levels and inadequate blood sugar control. Insulin resistance plays a primary and determining pathophysiological role in the onset and progression of the disease. \n\nManagement of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus relies on a combination of lifestyle interventions, including diet and exercise, alongside tailored pharmacological therapies to improve glycemic control. Metformin is established as the first-line treatment for the condition. Should further intervention be required, Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors are recommended as a second-line treatment following metformin. Additionally, agents such as sitagliptin and Zituvimet—which is indicated specifically for adult patients—are prescribed to optimize blood glucose management in conjunction with diet, exercise, and metformin regimens.\n\nTherapeutic approaches for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus also target associated cardiovascular and renal comorbidities to minimize complications. Patients with coronary heart disease (CHD) risk factors benefit from treatment with Atorvastatin Calcium, which effectively reduces the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke. Furthermore, in patients who present with renal complications such as albuminuria, the administration of Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors (ACEIs) or Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs) results in a significant decline in albuminuria levels.<SEP>A chronic metabolic condition characterized by elevated blood sugar levels, which can lead to life-threatening complications, reduced quality of life, and higher healthcare costs."}
{"entity": "National High Blood Pressure Education Program Joint National Committee", "type": "organization", "description": "An organization that publishes guidelines on prevention, detection, evaluation, and treatment of high blood pressure.<SEP>An organization that publishes guidelines on the prevention, detection, evaluation, and treatment of high blood pressure."}
{"entity": "ACE Inhibitors", "type": "concept", "description": "ACE Inhibitors are a class of cardiovascular medications that inhibit the angiotensin-converting enzyme, thereby altering the metabolism of bradykinin and eicosanoids while reducing aldosterone secretion. Common medications belonging to this class include lisinopril, enalapril, and ramipril. ACE Inhibitors are primarily utilized to lower blood pressure and slow the progression of renal disease.\n\nWhile effective, ACE Inhibitors carry several risks and drug interactions. By increasing serum potassium levels, they can trigger hyperkalemia or cause acute kidney injury, particularly when combined with other therapeutic agents. They are also associated with the risk of developing angioedema. Furthermore, when concomitantly administered with diabetes treatments such as Admelog (ADMELOG), ACE Inhibitors may increase the risk of hypoglycemia. Consequently, treatment guidelines often recommend initiating therapy at low initial doses and closely monitoring serum creatinine and potassium levels, particularly in patients suffering from chronic kidney disease.<SEP>Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, a therapeutic intervention used to treat patients with chronic kidney disease.<SEP>A class of medications, also known as angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, used to treat cardiovascular and renal conditions by reducing albuminuria progression."}
