{"entity": "Warfarin", "type": "artifact", "description": "Warfarin is a pharmaceutical chemical compound and widely prescribed anticoagulant medication primarily used to prevent the formation of blood clots. Characterized by a narrow therapeutic index, Warfarin requires careful clinical monitoring due to its complex pharmacological profile and its metabolism via cytochrome P450 enzymes. In patients suffering from chronic kidney disease, uremia-induced reduction in albumin binding causes an increase in the free fraction of Warfarin in the blood, altering its pharmacokinetics.\n\nAs a potent anticoagulant, Warfarin is associated with various contraindications, health risk factors, and a elevated potential for bleeding. This bleeding risk is significantly amplified when Warfarin is taken in combination with specific pharmaceutical agents. Concomitant use of Warfarin with aspirin or other antiplatelet drugs doubles or substantially increases the risk of bleeding, particularly when combined without specific clinical indications. Additionally, taking nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) alongside Warfarin significantly increases bleeding risks, and the medication exhibits a modest drug interaction when co-administered with omeprazole.<SEP>An anticoagulant medication evaluated in the systematic review for drug-drug interactions and bleeding risks.<SEP>An oral anticoagulant medicine used to prevent blood clots."}
