{"entity": "Hypertension", "type": "concept", "description": "Hypertension is a chronic medical condition characterized by persistently elevated or high blood pressure. If left unmanaged, the condition increases the overall risk of severe cardiovascular events. Additionally, in individuals diagnosed with non-valvular atrial fibrillation, hypertension significantly heightens the risk of stroke. It also plays a major role in complicating other health conditions, including chronic kidney disease and heart failure.\n\nThe management of hypertension relies on a combination of lifestyle interventions and targeted antihypertensive drug therapies designed to effectively lower blood pressure. Commonly prescribed therapeutic agents include amlodipine (and its formulation amlodipine besylate) as well as metoprolol tartrate. Treatment strategies are often tailored based on underlying comorbidities; for example, ACE inhibitors serve as first-line therapeutic agents when managing hypertension in patients with chronic kidney disease. Similarly, for patients with hypertension who also suffer from heart failure, carvedilol is slightly better suited as a treatment option compared to metoprolol."}
